<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>2.2.3: Graphs of Systems of Equations</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00382</md:content-id>
    <md:title>2.2.3: Graphs of Systems of Equations</md:title>
    <md:uuid>bff4f028-3dd5-4c6d-9cc4-c851fbf72792</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity (20 minutes)</title>
<para>This activity prompts students to write a system of equations from a graph, then reverse their steps to graph a system of equations by hand. Students will need to identify the slope and \(y\)-intercept from both the graph and an equation in \(y\)-intercept form. They will also need to recall that slope is the change in \(y\) over the change in \(x\). Finally, they will recognize that a solution to a system of equations is where the equations intersect on the graph.</para>
<title>Launch</title>
<para>Students will begin with a graph of the systems of equations and write the system from the graph. Facilitate a discussion about how to find slope and \(y\)-intercept from a graph. Have students share and compare their answers. </para>
<para>For Part II, put students in pairs. After students discuss with their partner, have a whole class discussion about working backward from a given system of equations to graph the system by hand. As a class, write the steps on the board. When graphing the equations, students may want to graph each equation with a different color on their coordinate plane. This is not a time to utilize technology as the goal is for students to graph by hand.</para>
<title>Student Activity</title>
<para>Now that you have written equations from tables, it is time to write systems of equations from graphs.</para>
<para><emphasis>Part 1: Writing a System of Equations from a Given Graph
  </emphasis></para>
<para>For questions 1 – 8, use the given graph:</para>
<para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/b54289584768c0c3eff29af44751b7ab9f385f7a"/></media></figure></para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item> For the green dashed line what is the \(y\)-value of the \(y\)-intercept? </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 9</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item> For the green dashed line what is the slope? </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> -1</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item> What is the equation of the green dashed line in slope-intercept form? </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(y= -x+9\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item> For the orange solid line what is the \(y\)-value of the \(y\)-intercept? </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 5</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item> For the orange solid line what is the slope? </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 3</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="6" type="enumerated">
  <item> What is the equation of the orange solid line in slope-intercept form? </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(y = 3x +5\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="7" type="enumerated">
  <item> Write the two equations as a system of linear equations. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis></para>
<para>\(y= -x+9\)</para>
<para>\(y = 3x +5\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="8" type="enumerated">
  <item> What is the solution of the system of equations? </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \((1, 8)\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Part 2: Use a Graph to Solve a Given System</emphasis></para>
<para>Now that you wrote a system of equations from a given graph, how can you work backward and graph a given system of equations by hand? Discuss this with a partner.</para>
<para>For questions 1 - 2, use this system of equations:</para>
<para>\(y=4x-4\)</para>
<para>\(y= -2x+8\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item> On the same coordinate plane, graph \(y = 4x -4\) and \(y = -2x +8\). (Students were provided access to Desmos.) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis></para>
<para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/122a63c57e6341129b9a0bc2d23dbe6ed6a955a3"/></media></figure></para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item> What is the solution to the system of equations? </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \((2, 4)\)</para>
<para>For numbers 3 and 4, consider the system of equations:</para>
<para>\(y = - \frac{3}{4}x+5\)</para>
<para>\(y=\frac{1}{2}x-5\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item> On the same coordinate plane, graph the system of equations. (Students were provided access to Desmos.) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> </para>
<para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/a4d952b331993f138b72346cb75349dcf901fc83"/></media></figure></para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item> What is the solution to the system of equations? </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \((8, -1)\)</para>

<title>Video: Graphing a System of Equations</title>
<para>Watch the following video to learn more about writing systems of equations from graphs.</para>
<div class="os-raise-d-flex-nowrap os-raise-justify-content-center">
  <media alt="Placeholder for Alt Text"><iframe width="660" height="371.4" src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/RQHhdqkJEo0?si=lGR1PpEOg-oREXv2;&amp;rel=0"/></media></div>


<title>Student Facing Extension</title>
<title>Are you ready for more?</title>
<para>Graph the following system of equations on a coordinate plane:</para>
<para>\(y=2x +5\)</para>
<para>\(4x-2y =10\)</para>
<para>Find the solution.</para>
<para>(Hint: For the second equation, solve for \(y\) first.)</para>
<para>(Students were provided access to Desmos.)</para>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Infinitely many solutions</para>
<para>When the second equation is solved for \(y\), it becomes \(-2y =-4x=10\) then \(y = 2x +5\). 
  Both equations are the same. 
  So, on the graph, one line is on top of the other and all solutions are possible.</para>
<title>Anticipated Misconceptions</title>
<para>Students may still have trouble mixing up the slope and \(y\)-intercept in slope-intercept form.</para>
<para>Additionally, when finding the slope, students may mix up the rise and run. Encourage students to draw on their graphs to emphasize the rise and run. In the extension, students may need to visually see that the lines are the same (or sitting on top of each other) and that multiple points are solutions to both equations.</para>
<title>Activity Synthesis</title>
<para>Encourage students to check their solutions to each system of equations into both of the equations to show that the solution works in both equations.</para>

<title>2.2.3: Self Check </title>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold"><emphasis>After the activity, students will answer the following question to check their understanding of the concepts explored in the activity.</emphasis></para>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">QUESTION:</para>
<para>Which of the following systems of equations is graphed:</para>
<figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/b6236f4d8bc85951d3f996b6232f8990e167f0af"/></media></figure><table class="os-raise-textheavytable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry><emphasis>Answers</emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>Feedback</emphasis></entry></row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>\(y=3x+4\)</para>
        <para>\(y=-3x+8\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: The slopes are mixed up. The correct answer is:</para>
        <para>\(y=-3x+4\)</para>
        <para>\(y=3x+8\)</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>\(y=-3x+4\)</para>
        <para>\(y=3x-8\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>That’s correct! Check yourself: The green line has a \(y\)-intercept at 4 and a slope of -3. The dashed line has a \(y\)-intercept of -8 and a slope of 3.</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>\(y=4x-4\)</para>
        <para>\(y=8x+3\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: In each equation, the slope and \(y\)-intercept need to switch. The correct answer is:</para>
        <para>\(y=-3x+4\)</para>
        <para>\(y=3x+8\)</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>\(y=3x+4\)</para>
        <para>\(y=3x+8\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: The green line is decreasing from left to right and has a negative slope. The correct answer is:</para>
        <para>\(y=-3x+4\)</para>
        <para>\(y=3x+8\)</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>


<title>2.2.3: Additional Resources</title>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold"><emphasis>The following content is available to students who would like more support based on their experience with the self check. Students will not automatically have access to this content, so you may wish to share it with those who could benefit from it.</emphasis></para>
<title>Solving Systems by Graphing</title>
<para>The graph of a linear equation is a line. Each point on the line is a solution to the equation. For a system of two equations, we will graph two lines. Then we can see all the points that are solutions to each equation. And, by finding what the lines have in common, we’ll find the solution to the system.</para>
<para>Most linear equations in one variable have one solution, but we saw that some equations, called contradictions, have no solutions and for other equations, called identities, all numbers are solutions.</para>
<para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/25738fe57c8edf5998507452567dc1f53431d373"/></media></figure></para>
<para><emphasis>Part 1: Writing a System of Equations from a Given Graph</emphasis></para>


<note>
  <para>How to write a system of linear equations from graphs.</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Find the slope and \(y\)-intercept of the first line.</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Find the slope and \(y\)-intercept of the second line. </para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Write the two equations for a system of equations.</para>
</note>
<para><emphasis>Example 1</emphasis></para>
<para>Write a system of equations from the graph.</para>
<para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/76a4914a606698e793a485badc7aa22305aa0210"/></media></figure></para>

<para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Find the slope and \(y\)-intercept of the \(\mathrm{\style{color:red}r}\mathrm{\style{color:red}e}\mathrm{\style{color:red}d}\) \(\mathrm{\style{color:red}d}\mathrm{\style{color:red}o}\mathrm{\style{color:red}t}\mathrm{\style{color:red}t}\mathrm{\style{color:red}e}\mathrm{\style{color:red}d}\) line.</para>
<para>\(m =\frac {(y_1-y_2)}{(x_1-x_2)}=\frac {(0-(-3))}{(6-0)}=\frac36=\frac12\)</para>
<para>\(y\)-intercept = \((0, -3)\)</para>

<para><emphasis>Step 2 </emphasis>- Find the slope and \(y\)-intercept of the \(\mathrm{\style{color:blue}{blue}}\) \(\mathrm{\style{color:blue}{solid}}\)  line.</para>
<para>\(m =\frac {(y_1-y_2)}{(x_1-x_2)}=\frac {(3-(-3))}{(3-0)}=\frac63=\frac21\)</para>
<para>\(y\)-intercept = \((0, -3)\)</para>

<para><emphasis>Step 3 </emphasis>- Write the two equations for a system of equations.</para>
<para>\(y = mx+b\)</para>
<para>\({\style{color:red}y} {\style{color:red}=}\frac {\style{color:red}1}{\style{color:red}2}{\style{color:red}x}{\style{color:red}-}{\style{color:red}3}\)</para>
<para>\({\style{color:blue}y} {\style{color:blue}=} {\style{color:blue}2}{\style{color:blue}x}{\style{color:blue}-}{\style{color:blue}3}\)</para>

<para><emphasis>Part 2: Use a Graph to Solve a Given System</emphasis></para>


<note>
  <para>How to solve a system of linear equations by graphing.</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Find the slope and \(y\)-intercept of the first equation.</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Find the slope and \(y\)-intercept of the second equation. </para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Graph the two equations on the same rectangular coordinate system.</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Estimate the solution to the system.</para>
</note>

<para>If the lines intersect, estimate the point of intersection. Check to make sure it is a solution to both equations. This is the solution to the system.</para>
<list>
  <item>If the lines are parallel, the system has no solution.</item>
  <item>If the lines are the same, the system has an infinite number of solutions.</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Example 2</emphasis></para>
<para>Solve the system by graphing: </para>
<para>\(y = 2x+1\)</para>
<para>\(y =4x-1\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Find the slope and \(y\)-intercept of the first equation.</para>
<para>In \(y=2x+1\), \(m=2\), \(b=1\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2 </emphasis>- Find the slope and \(y\)-intercept of the second equation. </para>
<para>In \(y=4x-1\), \(m=4\), \(b= -1\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3 </emphasis>- Graph the two equations on the same rectangular coordinate system.</para>
<para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/bd98a136209cf3f00ae2165c3d30bdf88f247d1c"/></media></figure></para>
<para><emphasis>Step 4 </emphasis>- Estimate the solution to the system.</para>
<para>The point where the lines intersect is:</para>
<para>\((1, 3)\)</para>
<para>Check to make sure the estimated point, \((1, 3)\) solves the system by substituting the values for \(x\) and \(y\) into both equations. </para>

<para>Check the first equation: \(𝑦=2𝑥+1\)</para>
<para>\(x = 1\)</para>
<para>\(y = 3\)</para>


  <para>\(y = 2x + 1\)</para>
 <para> \((3) = 2(1) + 1\)</para>
  <para>\(3 = 2 + 1\)</para>
  <para>\(3 = 3\)</para>
  <para>So, \((1, 3)\) solves \(y = 2x + 1\)</para>



<para>Check the second equation: \(𝑦=4𝑥−1\)</para>
<para>\(y = 4x - 1\)</para>
<para>\((3) = 4(1) - 1\)</para>
<para>\(3 = 4 - 1\)</para>
<para>\(3 = 3\)</para>
<para>So, \((1, 3)\) solves \(y = 4x - 1\)</para>

<title>Try It: Solving Systems by Graphing</title>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>Write a system of equations from the graph.</item>
</list>
<para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/016f624f1a4afa6b883f4e783357b7a095281a4f"/></media></figure></para>
<para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to check your work.</para>
<para><emphasis>Solution:</emphasis></para>
<para>Compare your answer:</para>
<para>Here is how to solve the system by graphing:</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1 </emphasis>-   Find the slope and \(y\)-intercept of the \(\mathrm{\style{color:red}r}\mathrm{\style{color:red}e}\mathrm{\style{color:red}d}\) \(\mathrm{\style{color:red}d}\mathrm{\style{color:red}o}\mathrm{\style{color:red}t}\mathrm{\style{color:red}t}\mathrm{\style{color:red}e}\mathrm{\style{color:red}d}\) line.</para>
<para>\(m =\frac {(y_1-y_2)}{(x_1-x_2)}=\frac {(0-(1))}{(2-0)}=\frac{-1}{2}=\frac{-1}{2}\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> -  Find the slope and \(y\)-intercept of the \(\mathrm{\style{color:blue}{blue}}\) \(\mathrm{\style{color:blue}{solid}}\)  line.</para>
<para>\(m =\frac {(y_1-y_2)}{(x_1-x_2)}=\frac {(1-(-3))}{(1-0)}=\frac{4}{1}\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Write the two equations for a system of equation.</para>
<para>\({\style{color:red}y} {\style{color:red}=}\frac {{\style{color:red}-}{\style{color:red}1}}{{\style{color:red}2}}{\style{color:red}x}{\style{color:red}+}{\style{color:red}1}\) </para>
<para>\({\style{color:blue}y} {\style{color:blue}=} {\style{color:blue}4}{\style{color:blue}x}{\style{color:blue}-}{\style{color:blue}3}\)</para>


<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>Solve the system by graphing:</item>
</list>
<para>\(y=3x+3\)</para>
<para>\(y= -x+7\)</para>
<para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to check your work.</para>
<para><emphasis>Solution:</emphasis></para>
<para>Here is how to solve the system by graphing:</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1 </emphasis>- Find the slope and \(y\)-intercept of the first equation.</para>
<para>In \(3x+3\), \(m=3\), \(b=3\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Find the slope and \(y\)-intercept of the second equation. </para>
<para>In \(y= -x+7\), \(m=-1\), \(b= 7\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Graph the two equations on the same rectangular coordinate system.</para>
<para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/7a4487fd1e1e26347257858e9fab62d5c33e6771"/></media></figure></para>
<para><emphasis>Step 4 </emphasis>- Estimate the solution to the system.</para>
<para>\((1, 6)\)</para>

</section>
